            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Neglecting the business side (contracts, invoicing) until problems arise</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">After onboarding hundreds of creators, OKAD sees the same toolkit mistakes repeated. Recognizing them upfront saves months of lost productivity and unnecessary spending.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">1. Tool hopping instead of mastering</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Every month a new editing app or AI tool goes viral. Creators who switch tools constantly never develop the muscle memory and speed that comes from deep familiarity. The creator who has mastered CapCut will always out-produce the creator who split their time across five different editors. Pick one editing app, learn its keyboard shortcuts, and use it until you genuinely hit its limits.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">2. Spending before earning</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">A $50/month tool subscription is a bad investment when you are earning $200/month. The Starter Stack exists for a reason: every tool you need to begin earning is free. Upgrade only when a paid tool will clearly save enough time to pay for itself. If a $15/month tool saves you 3 hours per month and you earn $50/hour, that is a 10x return. If it saves 15 minutes, it is a loss.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">3. Ignoring the business side</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Creative people gravitate toward creative tools and neglect operational ones. The most talented UGC creator on the planet still needs to invoice correctly, sign contracts, and track payments. OKAD has seen creators lose thousands of dollars to brands who never paid because there was no contract and no invoice &mdash; just a DM agreement. Set up your invoicing and contract tools before your first paid gig, not after your first dispute.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">4. No performance tracking</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Producing content without tracking results is like training without measuring progress. You cannot improve what you do not measure. Creators who maintain even a basic Google Sheet with hook rates and engagement metrics outperform creators who produce blindly. The sheet takes 5 minutes per video to update and provides the data you need to charge higher rates.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">5. Using personal accounts for business</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Mixing personal and business finances, email, and social accounts creates chaos as you scale. Set up a dedicated email address for brand communication, a separate PayPal or Wise account for payments, and a professional portfolio separate from your personal social profiles. The separation takes one afternoon to set up and prevents months of accounting headaches at tax time.</p>
